clueweb09-en0010-34-21544.0 Ask An Expert: Butter vs. Margarine Which is Better for You? [Providence Health and Services] Home | Health Plans | Providers | Programs & Services | Hospitals & Clinics | Health Info | Employment | Foundations Site Search Nutrition Center Ask an Expert Healthwise Library AudioLibrary myProvidence: Login Register Ask An Expert: Butter vs. Margarine Which is Better for You? Q: Years ago, I switched from butter to margarine to reduce my cholesterol intake. Now I hear that margarine contains something even worse than cholesterol – trans fat – so I’m thinking about switching back to butter. Weighing the pros and cons, which one really is the healthier choice: butter or margarine? Answer provided by Terese Scollard, MBA, R.D., L.D., regional clinical nutrition manager for Providence Nutrition Services: Butter contains a lot of artery-clogging saturated fat, and margarine contains an unhealthy combination of saturated and trans fats, so the healthiest choice is to skip both of them and use liquid oils, such as olive, canola and safflower oil, instead. However, even dietitians understand that some foods benefit greatly from a little buttery flavor; it wouldn’t be realistic to suggest that you give up butter and margarine altogether. If you want to use one or the other on occasion, margarine is the
